# Card

`Card` is the HQL type of a card. There is no `HmdCard`: the type carried an
`Hmd` prefix while card-ness was thought to follow from the file format, and it
does not, so the prefix named a condition the type no longer has.

A card is a [[doc-type|document]], narrowed:

```hql
type Card <: Doc {
    // the document represents a piece of knowledge
    // the document has a place in a vault
}
```

The narrowing conditions do not include the body's format. A card is a document
that gives a piece of knowledge a physical representation, and a plain Markdown
file can do that as readily as an `.hmd` one — see [[hmd-integration]].

Everything `Doc` provides — a `Data` header, a body, sections, anchors, links —
a card has, because a card is one. This page is about what a card has *in
addition*, and that addition is the point of the type.

> This revises an earlier direction on this card, which modelled a card as
> holding a `Doc` by composition, on the grounds that a document had no header
> to carry identity. Now that `Doc` carries a header of its own, the composition
> split has no work left to do and the relation is narrowing, the same mechanism
> used further down this page.

## A card is a document that knows where it is

A bare document is a file. A card is a file with a place in a **vault**, and
that place is what makes it addressable by name.

The vault boundary is HMD's. A namespace is a rooted tree of modules, and
resolution never crosses into a neighbouring vault: two vaults are two
namespaces. A card therefore has a name that other cards can write in double
brackets, a spine of ancestor modules that a bare name is probed against, and a
set of documents that may point at it.

This is what turns document machinery into card machinery:

- `[[alice]]` resolves, because there is a namespace to resolve it in.
- `downlinks` are answerable, because the vault knows what points at this card.
- `header.path` means something, because there is a root to be relative to.
- `import` and `use` in the header inherit or not, because there are ancestor
  folder notes to inherit from.

A plain Markdown file dropped into a vault becomes a card by acquiring that
context, not by being rewritten. That is the practical content of HMD's claim
that every `.md` file is already valid `.hmd`.

## A card is a knowledge participant

The difference that matters is not the file format. `card` is a term from
knowledge management, and a card is the kind of document that can reference
knowledge and operate on knowledge concepts.

```hql
knowledge : Knowledge
```

Knowledge is designed from day one, not deferred behind a release boundary. A
card's body, links, header and embeds are not merely content to be rendered:
they are the material that concepts, relations, propositions and evidence are
drawn from. A type system that treats cards as documents first and knowledge as
a later projection gets the dependency backwards, because the reason cards exist
is to carry meaning that a file system cannot.

Concretely, a card can:

- be a **concept** — an identified thing the vault has something to say about.
- be a **Relation Card** — a typed relationship promoted into a document, with
  its own identity, endpoints, evidence and incoming links.
- carry **propositions** in its body, which other cards may support or dispute.
- supply **evidence**, as ordinary sections with ordinary links.

None of this is automatic. A link is structural connectivity and does not assert
a semantic relation; a header entry is an author's claim and not a proof of
conformance. The path from document to knowledge runs through explicit
interpretation, and the rule used must be retained with the result. See
[[hql/knowledge]] for the semantic universe and what it retains: conflicting
claims, provenance, constraints and satisfaction.

`Doc` has no such capability and does not need one. A constructed body, an
embedded fragment or an ordinary Markdown file outside any vault is a perfectly
good document with nothing to say about knowledge. The narrowing is exactly the
step at which meaning becomes possible.

## A card's header

A card's header is a [[data-type|Data]] tree like any document's, assembled from
the same derived, authored and contributed layers. Two things are worth stating
here, though neither is what makes a document a card.

First, `header.format` is what licenses wikilinks, embeds and fragment
addressing in the body. A card written in `hmd` has them; a card written in `md`
has a header and a body like any other document, and no hyper layer to ask
about.

Second, where the body is `hmd`, HyperMarkDown reserves exactly four frontmatter
keys and leaves every other key to the author:

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `tags` | `/`-separated hierarchical strings; categorize, but do not resolve |
| `use` | Per-card feature toggles; inherits from the nearest ancestor folder note |
| `import` | Name bindings and search origins; applies to this card only, never inherits |
| `nav` | `order` and `visibility` for publication |

Projecting these as stronger HQL types is sound, because HMD validates them. The
rest of the tree stays `Data`, and that openness is deliberate: a type system
with a declared `Frontmatter` record would have to grow a field every time a
vault invented a key.

```hql
card.header.tags
card.header.nav.visibility
```

What a card whose frontmatter fails to parse projects to is unresolved — a card
with an empty header, a card carrying a diagnostic, or no card at all.

## cards

`cards` is a reserved keyword. It denotes every card in the current namespace
vault:

```hql
cards : List<Card>
```

This is a context value, not a function call and not a filesystem sweep written
by the user. It has the same horizon a wikilink does: `cards` in one vault never
contains a card from another.

```hql
cards
| typed Relation
| graph
```

Being a keyword rather than a binding means a card cannot shadow it by being
named `cards`, and that a program never has to state where the vault is. What
`cards` means inside a nested module — the whole vault, or the module subtree —
still needs a decision, as does whether imported namespaces contribute to it.

There is no matching keyword for documents, and there should not be. `cards` is
bounded because a vault is bounded; documents in general are not a collection
anything can enumerate.

> The collection type is unsettled. This card states `List<Card>`, while
> [[hql/collections]] records `Set<Card>`, on the grounds that discovering files
> must not establish an ordering guarantee. The two pages have to be reconciled
> by a decision about whether vault order is observable, not by editing one page
> to match the other. Tracked as `CON-05` in
> [consolidation](../../../status/consolidation.md).

## Extensions extend the header

The header is the extension boundary, and an [[hql/extensions|extension]] can
extend it in two quite different senses. Keeping them apart matters.

**Contributing entries.** Some extensions know facts the file does not state — a
`git` extension supplying authorship and revision, an indexer supplying
embeddings or a semantic-search score, a link checker supplying resolution
diagnostics, a build supplying publication state.

```hql
git.metadata(card) : Data
```

This is the second reason the header is broader than frontmatter: most of what a
mature vault knows about a card was never typed into the card.

**Contributing type knowledge.** Other extensions add nothing to the tree at all.
They declare what an existing region of it means, so a subtree that was `Data`
becomes usable at a stronger type without being converted or copied.

```hql
import knowledge

card.header.knowledge          : Data?   // before
card.header.knowledge          : KnowledgeMetadata?
card.header.knowledge.concepts : List<Concept>
```

The runtime value is unchanged; what changed is what the checker can prove about
it. An extension owns type knowledge about a header, not the header itself —
[[refinement-of-types]] develops this as open structural data with progressive
refinement, and it is the mechanism behind the narrowing below.

The core's obligation is to keep contributions distinguishable. A header entry
that was derived, authored or contributed is not interchangeable with the
others, and provenance records where a value came from rather than conferring
authority on it. How contributed entries are namespaced, whether an extension
may overwrite an author's key, and what happens when two extensions contribute
the same path are open — though an extension that only contributes type
knowledge raises none of those questions, which is an argument for preferring
that form.

> [[refinement-of-types]] spells this field `card.metadata`. This page and
> [[doc-type]] spell it `card.header`, on the grounds that a header is a
> document-level notion and `metadata` says less about where it sits. The two
> pages describe the same field and the spelling still has to be settled in one
> place.

## Types narrow

`Card <: Doc` is the first narrowing, not a special case. The same mechanism
continues past it, and it is the same mechanism by which a `Data` subtree
becomes a known shape — see [[refinement-of-types]].

```hql
type Relation[S, T] <: Card {
    source: Ref[S]
    target: Ref[T]
}

type WorksOn <: Relation[Person, Project] {
    since: Date?
    role: Role?
}
```

Declaration syntax is illustrative. The direction is not: a Relation Card is a
card, so it keeps its body, its links and its own identity, and other cards can
link to it. Narrowing adds guarantees; it never replaces the card with a record.
See [[hql/knowledge]] for why significant relationships are preferably cards.

Narrowing has to be checked. A `type: WorksOn` entry in the header is an author's
claim, and `typed Relation` must verify the contract rather than trust the label
— an unchecked `as` would let any YAML key mint a type. The result of a failed
narrowing, the spelling of validation and refinement, and whether narrowing is
nominal or structural remain open.

```hql
person : Person = validate[Person](card)
```

Narrowing is also how a vault stays usable while it is being typed. A document
that satisfies nothing beyond `Doc` is still a first-class value; a card that
satisfies nothing beyond `Card` is still in `cards` and still traversable.
Stronger types are earned where there is a reason for them, and the untyped
remainder is not an error.

See [[doc-type]] for the document a card narrows, [[data-type]] for the header's
tree type, [[hql/knowledge]] for the semantic universe, [[pipes]] for composition
and [[hql/design-status]] for what is still undecided.
